------------------------------------------
- How to Declare and Create ArrayLists? -
------------------------------------------

In order to be able to use an ArrayList in our Java programs, we must first import the necessary package from the 
Java standard library.

import java.util.ArrayList;

Now that we have imported Java's ArrayList class into our project, we can declare and instantiate an ArrayList. The syntax for 
declaring and instantiating an ArrayList is a bit different from other declarations and instantiations that you have seen so 
far in this course. This is because ArrayList is what is known as a generic type in Java.

---------------------------------------------------------
	Generic Types
---------------------------------------------------------

Generic types in Java are a kind of class (we will learn more about classes soon) that can be used to instantiate specialized 
objects of the generic type. An ArrayList, like an array, is a kind of container for other types. An array of integers (int[ ])
is a container for int values, and an array of strings (String[ ]) is a container for String references. Each array is 
specialized to store some type of data. The ArrayList class is generic - it is not specialized to store values of any specific 
type. If we want to declare and create an ArrayList to store values of a particular type, then we must specify that type when 
we declare and instantiate the ArrayList.

Let's look at an example, in which we declare and instantiate an ArrayList that is specialized to store String references. Take 
a moment or two to look closely at the line of code below. What is different about this declaration and instantiation? 
How is it syntactically different from other declarations and instantiations that you have previously seen in Java code?

ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<String>();

Undoubtedly, you noticed the angle brackets (< >) with the data type String inside of them. In simple terms, this 
ArrayList<String> is the full data type name for the specialized type of ArrayList that we are declaring and instantiating -
we are declaring an ArrayList of Strings (ArrayList<String>).

---------------------------------------------------------
	ArrayList Variable Declaration
---------------------------------------------------------

All specialized ArrayList variable declarations look the same in Java. Such declarations always consist of the datatype name 
ArrayList followed by a pair of angle brackets that contains the data type of the elements, followed by the name of the 
variable we are declaring. The pair of angle brackets with the type name inside is called a type parameter.

ArrayList<elementType> arraylistName


ArrayList<String> names

-----------------------------------------------------------
	ArrayList Object Instantiation
-----------------------------------------------------------

All specialized ArrayList object instantiations look the same in Java. Such instantiations always consist of the keyword new, 
followed by the datatype name ArrayList, followed by a pair of angle brackets that contains the data type of the elements 
(called a type parameter), followed by a pair of parentheses.

new ArrayList<elementType>()


new ArrayList<String>()


Notice that, unlike an array, when we instantiate an ArrayList we do not need to specify a size. Remember that,
by default, an ArrayList starts out as an empty container, and they will automatically grow and shrink as needed.
